{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 3,
  "simulation_id": "sim-03",
  "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B014",
        "laureates": [
          "Antoine Georges",
          "Dieter Vollhardt",
          "Gabriel Kotliar"
        ],
        "citation": "for dynamical mean-field theory of strongly correlated electrons"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B025",
    "B001"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "DMFT is an unusually consequential theoretical advance: it made correlation-driven electronic phenomena quantitatively tractable and established a durable bridge between many-body models and calculations for real materials. Its value is not merely methodological; decades of applications, extensions, and comparisons with material properties have made it a common language for correlated-electron physics. The proposed trio gives a coherent account of the foundational construction and its formulation into a broadly usable physical framework.",
    "case_against": "The historical boundary between the original infinite-dimensional construction, the self-consistent impurity formulation, and later practical extensions needs exact scrutiny. A prize for a framework must also make clear which verified physical insight, rather than general computational usefulness, is being recognized.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig emphasizes that DMFT transformed strongly correlated materials research but notes that credit extends beyond the three proposed names.",
        "response": "I agree that the lineage is broader and that this is the principal attribution issue. Nevertheless, Vollhardt, Georges, and Kotliar remain a defensible compact core if the citation is confined to the foundational theory rather than its many later implementations."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson regards optical lattice clocks as a mature, precisely attributable advance combining quantum control with major metrological consequences.",
        "response": "That is a strong alternative: the independent reproducibility and two-person attribution are exemplary. I would, however, retain DMFT at this stage because it supplied a more fundamental and widely tested account of an important class of material phenomena; B001 should remain under close consideration."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson stresses that topological insulators unite defining quantum-spin-Hall theory with a landmark experimental realization, while requiring careful scope relative to related topological phases.",
        "response": "I share the assessment of B025 as a compelling new state of matter with unusually clear theory-experiment interplay. Its relation to the broader topological-matter landscape is nevertheless a real delimitation problem, whereas the conceptual scope of DMFT can be stated more narrowly."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether archival priority supports exactly these three names for the foundational formulation rather than a differently balanced allocation.",
      "How narrowly a citation can distinguish DMFT's physical breakthrough from later electronic-structure and computational extensions."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
